<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Orange</h1><p class="st-meta">1163 CE – 1713 CE · capital Orange</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A tiny sovereign principality in the Rhône valley whose title passed to the house of Nassau and gave the Dutch royal family its name.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Orange was a Roman colony and later a county within the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-arles">Kingdom of Arles</a>; the troubadour <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raimbaut-d-orange">Raimbaut d'Orange</a> ruled part of it in the 12th century. In 1163 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Emperor Frederick Barbarossa</a> raised it to a principality, and it passed in 1173 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bertrand-of-baux">Bertrand of Baux</a>, whose family ruled it for over two centuries.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The principality was a small enclave inside <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-provence">Provence</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/comtat-venaissin">Comtat Venaissin</a>, with Roman monuments, a university founded in 1365 and its own courts. It passed to the Chalon family in 1393. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rene-of-chalon">René of Chalon</a> left it to his cousin William of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-nassau">Nassau</a> in 1544, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-silent">William the Silent</a> Orange became a refuge for Protestants.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> occupied Orange several times and destroyed its fortifications. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-iii-of-england">William III of England</a> in 1702, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> took it, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-utrecht">Treaty of Utrecht</a> in 1713 recognised its annexation. The princes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-orange-nassau">Orange-Nassau</a> kept the title, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> royal house still bears.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-orange.jpg" alt="Principality of Orange" width="320" height="224"><figcaption>Image: Jan Jansson (1588-1664) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Principality_of_Orange_(1633).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1163 CE – 1713 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Orange</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bertrand of Baux, René of Chalon, William the Silent</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity and Calvinism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Occitan and French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Orange, County of Orange</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1438&amp;focus=principality-of-orange" aria-label="Principality of Orange on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4795 615 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Orange lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5115 871L5229 694M5115 871L5097 634M5115 871L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5097 634h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5115 871h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5115 871h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Orange. <a href="/atlas?year=1438&amp;focus=principality-of-orange">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
